.TH nut_webgui 7 "__PLACEHOLDER_DATE" "__PLACEHOLDER_VERSION" "nut_webgui User Manual"
.SH NAME
.PP
nut_webgui \- configuring server

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B nut_webgui
is an alternative to
.MR upsstats.cgi 8 .
It displays UPS device stats from multiple
.MR upsd 8
servers.
.PP
In addition to the web interface, nut_webgui provides data endpoints for custom
integrations:
.TP
.BR /api
Return UPS device data in JSON format.
.TP
.BR /metrics
Exposes device metrics in OpenMetrics 1.0 format.
.TP
.BR /events
Streams real\-time events via a WebSocket connection.
.TP
.BR /probes
Provides basic health check endpoints.
.PP
These APIs are primarily intended for programming helper tools for logging and
visualization, rather than as an
.MR upsmon 8
replacement. For more detailed API usage, see the online documentation.

.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
.MR nut_webgui 1
can be configured via args, environment variables, or config file. All
configuration options are merged into a single unified config based on their
priority.

Config priority order highest to lowest:

.RS
1. CLI args
.br
2. Environment variables
.br
3. config.toml file
.RE
.PP
For CLI args and environment variables, see the
.MR nut_webgui 1
man page.

.SH CONFIG.TOML
.PP
The config file is a TOML v1.1.0 formatted document that allows configuring
the HTTP server,
.MR upsd 8
server connections, authentication, etc.

.PP
The default config file location is
.I /etc/nut_webgui/config.toml
for most installation methods. However, this is not a requirement and the config
file location can be changed to any path with the
.I \-\-config\-file
argument.

.PP
config.toml template is also available at
.I /usr/local/share/nut_webgui/config.toml

.SS EXAMPLE
.PP
The following config.toml example shows two
.MR upsd 8
server connections, authentication, and HTTP server settings.

.EX
        version = "1"
        log_level = "info"
        default_theme = "tokyo-night"

        [http_server]
        base_path = "/"
        listen = "0.0.0.0"
        port = 9000
        worker_count = 8

        [upsd.default]
        username = "admin"
        password = "Pass0rd"
        address = "localhost"
        port = 3493
        max_connection = 4
        poll_freq = 30
        poll_interval = 2
        tls_mode = "disable"

        [upsd.reactor]
        address = "10.0.12.10"
        username = "observer"
        password = "AbsoluteSecurity"

        [auth]
        users_file = "/etc/nut_webgui/users.toml"
        allow_anonymous_metrics = false
.EE

.SH AUTHENTICATION
.PP
By default,
.MR nut_webgui 1
server can be accessed without authentication. Authentication can be enabled by
providing a
.I users.toml
file via configuration options.
.PP
Once authentication is enabled:
.RS
- Web UI requires session cookies to access the pages.
.br
- Both the Data API and WebSocket event streams require API keys to access data.
.br
- Metrics endpoint requires API key unless anonymous metrics flag is enabled.
.RE
.SS SERVER KEY
.PP
All user sessions and API keys are signed with a server key. Changing this key
invalidates all existing user sessions and API keys.
.PP
For multi-instance deployments (e.g., behind a load balancer), all instances
must use the same server key.
.SS USERS FILE
.PP
user file is a simple TOML\-formatted file where each user is defined as a table.
.SS EXAMPLE
.EX
        [username]
        password = "asdf"
        permissions = ["setvar", "instcmd", "fsd"]

        [username2]
        password = "passw0rd"

        [spear-of-democracy]
        password = "down-up-right-up-left-right"
        permissions = ["instcmd"]

        [sector-g-admin]
        password = "otis123"
        permissions = ["fsd"]
.EE
.SS USER PERMISSIONS
nut_webgui has three different user permissions:
.TP
.BR setvar
User can change UPS variables.
.TP
.BR instcmd
User can call instant commands on the target UPS.
.TP
.BR fsd
User can force shutdown of the target UPS.

.SH TLS MODES
.PP
Secure communication between
.MR nut_webgui 1
and
.MR upsd 8
servers can be configured via the
.I tls_mode
option.
.MR nut_webgui 1
uses openssl-prober to check system certificates located in the
.I /etc/ssl/certs/
directory. Make sure the required root certificates are present at this location.
.PP
Available TLS modes are:

.TP
.BR disable
UPSD connection uses plain text communication and no TLS is enabled.
.TP
.BR strict
UPSD connection is secured by TLS, and nut_webgui refuses to connect if
certificate verification fails.
.TP
.BR skip
UPSD connection is secured by TLS, and nut_webgui ignores any certificate
verification issues. This option is only recommended for testing
self-signed certificates.

